# Files loaded in the order of the list. Separator = backslash, never a slash.
# Core/Locale.lua is listed FIRST among the Core/ modules: the language layer is
# a dependency of Config, Intermission, Panel and UI.
# Core/Sound.lua comes right AFTER Locale.lua and BEFORE Config.lua: it owns the
# pure table "canonical state -> soundboard file", the one-playback-per-assignment
# gate and the BOUNDED preference resolved by Config.resolveIntermission
# (/gr sound on|off). It depends on nothing else, so it stays out of the way of
# the module order below.
# Core/BossFilter.lua also comes BEFORE Config.lua: Config resolves the auto-open
# allow-list (encounter ids + names, `/gr boss <id>`) with it, and the UI layer
# asks it WHICH boss may open the panel by itself (the DELIVERED default target is
# encounter id 3445, with the English and French names as a secondary criterion).
# Core/Diag.lua comes right AFTER Core/BossFilter.lua: it builds the report of
# `/gr diag` (the four sound files + the effective target + the idlog + the ping)
# and depends only on Locale and Sound, both already loaded. It contains NO client
# call: the rendering layer (UI/Panel.lua) injects the CVars and the answers of
# PlaySoundFile into it.
# Core/Diag.lua is PURE: it never plays a sound itself. The silence gate it owns
# (ProbeGate) is what keeps `/gr diag` from making any noise in a raid.
# Core/Config.lua holds the DELIVERED DEFAULT of the auto-open target
# (Config.DEFAULT_BOSS_IDS / Config.DEFAULT_BOSS_NAMES): encounter id 3445,
# measured in game by the raid lead on 2026-09-24 (heroic pull, 20 players), plus
# the English and French names of the boss. Every difficulty of that boss opens the
# panel: the difficulty is deliberately NOT part of the filter.
# Core/Simulation.lua comes AFTER Core/Intermission.lua (it reuses the canonical
# states, the ping labels and the binding candidates) and BEFORE the UI/ files.
# Core/Textures.lua comes right AFTER Core/Simulation.lua and BEFORE
# Core/Layout.lua: it owns the pure table "canonical state -> texture file (TGA)"
# of the three image buttons of the intermission panel (the raid lead's
# screenshots of the orbs), and the fitted size of each file - the layout needs
# both to size and to fill the buttons.
# Core/Layout.lua comes LAST among the Core/ modules: it computes the DISPOSITION
# of the panels (pure geometry, measured from Core/Locale strings) and the UI/
# files apply it as-is.
# THE STYLE SHOWCASE (UI/Showcase.lua) comes LAST of all: it asks UI/Intermission.lua
# whether a real fight is running (it owns the only way into the showcase, and the
# showcase must never open during an intermission).
# Bindings.xml is NOT listed here: the client loads it automatically
# (https://warcraft.wiki.gg/wiki/Creating_key_bindings).
GideonRaid.lua
Core\Locale.lua
Core\Sound.lua
Core\BossFilter.lua
Core\Diag.lua
Core\Config.lua
Core\Pairing.lua
Core\Intermission.lua
Core\Simulation.lua
Core\Textures.lua
Core\Layout.lua
UI\Panel.lua
UI\Intermission.lua
UI\Showcase.lua

# SOUNDBOARDS OF AN ASSIGNMENT (one file per canonical state, played ONCE when
# the player declares their composition) AND THE INTERMISSION START SOUND (played
# ONCE at the very beginning of every intermission, i.e. when the panel opens by
# itself 2 s before the intermission). THEY MUST BE LISTED HERE: the client does
# NOT load a sound file that is not listed in the .toc, and an unlisted file makes
# PlaySoundFile fail silently.
# The three assignment files are SILENT PLACEHOLDERS (0.2 s of silence, Ogg
# Vorbis) until the raid lead delivers the real recordings. REPLACING THEM IS A
# FILE DROP: same names, same folder (Sound/), same format (Ogg Vorbis), no code
# change - see README.md, section "Replacing the three sounds".
# intermission-start.ogg IS the raid lead's own recording (stereo 44.1 kHz,
# 3.22 s): it must NOT be renamed, re-encoded nor moved, and it is played once per
# intermission (see Core/Sound.lua, takeIntermissionStart).
Sound\assign-1v3r.ogg
Sound\assign-2v2r.ogg
Sound\assign-3v1r.ogg
Sound\intermission-start.ogg

# TEXTURES OF THE THREE CARDS (the raid lead's screenshots of the orbs).
# THEY MUST BE LISTED HERE, exactly like the sounds: a texture loaded by PATH
# ("Interface\AddOns\GideonRaid\Texture\3v1r.tga") and NOT listed in the .toc is
# not loaded by the client, and the card then draws nothing at all.
# THEY ARE NOT IN assets/: that folder is EXCLUDED from the packaged zip
# (.pkgmeta, `ignore: assets`) and is only there for the project documentation.
# The files are produced by tools/make_textures.py (RGBA PNG -> 32-bit
# uncompressed TGA, aspect ratio and alpha preserved, fitted in a 256 px box):
# REPLACING THEM IS A FILE DROP - same names, same folder (Texture/), same
# format (uncompressed 32-bit TGA), no code change. A new size has to be
# reported in Core/Textures.lua (Textures.SIZES_BY_STATE): the tool prints the
# exact block, and tests/spec/texture_spec.lua compares it with the real header
# of every file.
Texture\1v3r.tga
Texture\2v2r.tga
Texture\3v1r.tga

# THE ILLUSTRATION OF THE PLACEMENT PANEL (the drawing the raid lead delivered).
# It is NOT a state: it never appears during a fight, it is the reference the
# player uses to see the size and the spot the window will occupy (/gr inter
# place, "PLACE INTERMISSION PANEL"). Same rules as the three cards: a 32-bit
# uncompressed TGA in Texture/ (never in assets/), listed HERE (a texture loaded
# by path and not listed is not loaded at all), produced by
# tools/make_textures.py from the delivered PNG, fitted in a 384 px box with the
# aspect ratio and the alpha preserved (Textures.PLACEMENT_SIZE reports the real
# size, tests/spec/texture_spec.lua compares it with the header of the file).
Texture\placement.tga

# THE GIDEON FRAME (the 9-slice border of the "GIDEON" card style, a CANDIDATE the
# raid lead previews in the style showcase with /gr sim style gideon). Same rules
# as every other texture: a 32-bit uncompressed TGA in Texture/, listed HERE (an
# unlisted texture loaded by path is not loaded at all), and its real size is
# compared with the header of the file by tests/spec/texture_spec.lua.
# IT IS GENERATED, NOT HAND-MADE: tools/make_gideon_frame.py writes it from pure
# arithmetic (no Pillow, no source image) and can verify it byte for byte:
#     python3 tools/make_gideon_frame.py --check
# It is WHITE on purpose (a 2 px border + a soft inner halo, 64x64): the colour
# comes from the game (SetBackdropBorderColor), so the style is pure DATA - gold at
# rest, CYAN on hover, bright gold on press.
Texture\gideon-frame.tga
